RULES='''You are navigating an unknown mirror-wall maze. The host automatically attaches the current first-person images to EVERY planning inference. You do not need to fetch observations and must not call any tools to get them. You are not given a map or coordinates.
Action space: left, right, forward. left/right rotate one grid direction (square90 degrees, hexagon60 degrees). forward moves one adjacent cell center while preserving heading. A closed wall blocks forward movement. Closed walls are mirrors (up to10 reflections), so reflected paths and green beacons may be virtual. Floors and ceilings are matte. The robot's orange chest/visor/head arrow marks its front; the back is blue. Enter the ACTUAL green beacon cell to succeed.
Objective, in priority order: reach the goal in as FEW PLANNING DECISIONS as possible, then minimize collisions. At most200 planning decisions. One response is one decision and may contain multiple actions. There is no action-sequence length cap. After your sequence, the next turn includes the image after EVERY attempted action, in order, including rotations and wall collisions; the LAST image is the current state. All valid actions execute even after a collision, stopping if the goal is reached. Illegal sequences are rejected as a whole with no movement and count as ONE collision.
For each turn, inspect the attached images and return ONE JSON object {"plan":"brief navigation intention","actions":["left","forward"]}. Keep the intention brief, not private chain-of-thought. A nonempty JSON action array is also accepted. Only exact lowercase left/right/forward strings are legal. Do not ask questions, use shell/files/browser/web/other agents, request a map, or try to execute actions yourself. The host executes the plan. Return this turn's plan now, not an entire autonomous tool-running loop. If your harness requires its native finish wrapper, you may use it only to return the JSON; it is not a maze action. Do not claim success on your own; the host verifies it.'''

